# Tracer: native `Set α` carriers and context-free simple set objects.
#
# Before:
# union(A, B) could verify in Litex, but To-Lean had no structural Obj IR for
# it. The target prelude also used `LitexSet := Type uLitex`, which made a set
# parameter a Lean type rather than a value in Litex's one object universe.
#
# Now:
# A and B share one implicit element carrier `α` and become native `Set α`
# values. Each constructor preserves source argument order and maps to the
# corresponding Mathlib operation; reflexive equality is replayed by checked
# `rfl` evidence.
forall A, B set:
    union(A, B) = union(A, B)

forall A, B set:
    intersect(A, B) = intersect(A, B)

forall A, B set:
    set_minus(A, B) = set_minus(A, B)

# Boundary:
# A set-builder owns a binder, its carrier, local facts, and local symbol scope.
# It remains unsupported until To-Lean has binder-aware Obj IR. The focused Rust
# regression constructs that node and requires IR construction to reject it.
#
# Evidence:
# target/release/litex -compact -isolated -runner -f examples/05_compiler_interop/to_lean_set_obj_abi.lit
# Focused compiler gate:
# cargo test --release to_lean_set_obj_abi -- --nocapture
# Generated Mathlib/Lean kernel gate:
# LITEX_LEAN_PROJECT=/path/to/mathlib cargo test --release generated_to_lean_set_obj_abi_compiles_with_lean -- --ignored --nocapture
# Implementation: src/to_lean_ir/obj.rs, src/runtime/runtime_to_lean_ir.rs,
# src/to_lean/type_context.rs, src/to_lean/set_prelude.rs, and
# src/to_lean/to_lean_pipeline.rs
